WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.952
Now the running time.

00:00:02.030 --> 00:00:05.412
Partition on a range of m elements compares

00:00:05.491 --> 00:00:08.479
each of the other m - 1 elements

00:00:08.558 --> 00:00:11.783
with the pivot once, so it costs Θ(m).

00:00:11.861 --> 00:00:14.299
The total depends on how evenly

00:00:14.378 --> 00:00:16.580
the pivots split the ranges.

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:19.936
The worst split is maximally unbalanced

00:00:19.936 --> 00:00:21.364
one side is empty

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:24.070
and the other has all the remaining

00:00:24.145 --> 00:00:25.724
n - 1 elements.

00:00:25.799 --> 00:00:28.354
That happens when the pivot is the

00:00:28.429 --> 00:00:31.661
smallest or the largest value; for example

00:00:31.736 --> 00:00:34.818
when the last element is always the pivot

00:00:34.893 --> 00:00:37.298
and the input is already sorted.

00:00:37.298 --> 00:00:39.853
The next level costs n - 1

00:00:39.853 --> 00:00:43.010
then n - 2, and so on, down to 2.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:46.926
The recursion tree is a path of depth n

00:00:46.926 --> 00:00:50.848
and the costs add up to

00:00:50.848 --> 00:00:52.720
n(n + 1)/2: quadratic.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.892
The best split is balanced

00:00:55.892 --> 00:01:00.020
both sides have about n / 2 elements.

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:03.476
Then T(n) is 2T(n / 2)

00:01:03.476 --> 00:01:04.820
plus O(n)

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:07.939
the same recurrence as merge sort.

00:01:07.939 --> 00:01:11.360
Every level of the tree costs n in total

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:14.210
and there are log₂ n levels.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.932
So the best case is n log n.

00:01:17.016 --> 00:01:19.473
But surely we cannot hope for

00:01:19.558 --> 00:01:21.760
perfect splits every time.

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:25.182
What about a split that looks quite bad?

00:01:25.253 --> 00:01:28.095
Suppose every partition puts 10%

00:01:28.166 --> 00:01:30.155
of the elements on one side

00:01:30.155 --> 00:01:32.500
and 90% on the other.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:34.563
In the recursion tree

00:01:34.563 --> 00:01:37.055
every full level still costs n

00:01:37.133 --> 00:01:40.015
each level splits the same n elements

00:01:40.092 --> 00:01:41.650
into smaller pieces.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.478
The shortest path keeps taking

00:01:44.561 --> 00:01:47.287
the 1/10 side and ends after

00:01:47.369 --> 00:01:49.434
log₁₀ n levels.

00:01:49.517 --> 00:01:51.500
The longest keeps taking

00:01:51.582 --> 00:01:53.317
the 9/10 side

00:01:53.399 --> 00:01:56.208
and ends after

00:01:56.291 --> 00:01:59.760
log_{10/9} n levels; deeper levels cost at most n.

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:03.089
Both bounds are constants times n log n

00:02:03.089 --> 00:02:05.322
so this split is n log n too.

00:02:05.322 --> 00:02:08.599
The same works for any constant proportion

00:02:08.599 --> 00:02:10.758
99 to 1 included.

00:02:10.833 --> 00:02:14.035
Only splits that peel off a constant number

00:02:14.109 --> 00:02:16.790
of elements per level are quadratic.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.716
Now the expected time of

00:02:19.814 --> 00:02:21.867
randomized quicksort.

00:02:21.965 --> 00:02:24.995
Elements are only ever compared

00:02:25.093 --> 00:02:28.808
with a pivot, so we count comparisons.

00:02:28.808 --> 00:02:30.568
Let e_1, e_2

00:02:30.568 --> 00:02:34.772
up to e_n be the elements in sorted order.

00:02:34.772 --> 00:02:37.802
When are e_i and e_j compared?

00:02:37.802 --> 00:02:40.148
Take e_3 and e_6

00:02:40.148 --> 00:02:43.570
and the set from e_3 to e_6.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:45.836
Until some element of this

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:47.753
set is chosen as a pivot

00:02:47.753 --> 00:02:50.480
they all stay together in one range.

00:02:50.553 --> 00:02:52.838
If the first pivot from the set

00:02:52.912 --> 00:02:55.786
lies strictly between them, say e_4

00:02:55.786 --> 00:02:59.103
then e_3 goes left and e_6 goes right

00:02:59.103 --> 00:03:01.240
and they are never compared.

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:03.992
If the first pivot from the set

00:03:04.069 --> 00:03:06.161
is e_3 or e_6 itself

00:03:06.161 --> 00:03:08.950
the two are compared, exactly once.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:12.384
So e_i and e_j are compared if and

00:03:12.474 --> 00:03:15.376
only if one of them is the first

00:03:15.467 --> 00:03:17.916
of e_i to e_j to be picked.

00:03:18.006 --> 00:03:21.543
Each of the j − i + 1 elements

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:24.355
is equally likely to be first

00:03:24.445 --> 00:03:26.713
so the probability is

00:03:26.803 --> 00:03:28.980
2/(j − i + 1).

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:31.693
Neighbours are always compared

00:03:31.769 --> 00:03:34.513
the smallest and largest of 10 only

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:36.876
with probability 2/10.

00:03:36.876 --> 00:03:40.002
A simulation agrees: 0.201

00:03:40.002 --> 00:03:41.450
against 0.2.

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:45.506
Add it up with indicator random variables.

00:03:45.506 --> 00:03:48.982
X_{i,j} is 1 if e_i and e_j are compared

00:03:48.982 --> 00:03:50.637
and 0 otherwise

00:03:50.637 --> 00:03:54.030
its expectation is just the probability

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:56.430
2/(j − i + 1).

00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:59.745
Each pair is compared at most once

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:02.711
so the total number of comparisons

00:04:02.796 --> 00:04:05.168
is the sum of all the X_{i,j}.

00:04:05.168 --> 00:04:07.625
By linearity of expectation

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:10.336
the expectation of a sum is the

00:04:10.421 --> 00:04:12.455
sum of the expectations

00:04:12.455 --> 00:04:15.420
even when the terms are dependent.

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:18.662
Group the pairs by their distance x

00:04:18.662 --> 00:04:20.268
which is j − i.

00:04:20.268 --> 00:04:22.839
Distance x has n − x pairs

00:04:22.839 --> 00:04:25.650
and the sum splits into two parts.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.097
What is left is the harmonic series

00:04:29.185 --> 00:04:31.220
without its first term

00:04:31.309 --> 00:04:33.609
and H_n is

00:04:33.698 --> 00:04:35.556
ln n plus a constant.

00:04:35.644 --> 00:04:38.918
So the expected number of comparisons

00:04:39.007 --> 00:04:42.015
is 2(n+1)H_n

00:04:42.015 --> 00:04:44.581
minus 4n: O(n log n)

00:04:44.581 --> 00:04:48.120
about 1.39 n log₂ n.

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:51.570
This program counts the comparisons

00:04:51.652 --> 00:04:54.769
on sorted input, for both pivot rules.

00:04:54.769 --> 00:04:58.213
The columns are n, the last-element rule

00:04:58.295 --> 00:05:00.264
the random rule averaged

00:05:00.346 --> 00:05:03.380
over 100 runs, and the formula.

00:05:03.730 --> 00:05:05.538
For 1000 elements

00:05:05.614 --> 00:05:08.251
the last-element rule makes exactly

00:05:08.326 --> 00:05:10.511
n(n − 1)/2,

00:05:10.587 --> 00:05:13.073
499,500

00:05:13.149 --> 00:05:15.032
comparisons.

00:05:15.108 --> 00:05:16.916
The random rule averages

00:05:16.991 --> 00:05:18.574
close to the formula

00:05:18.574 --> 00:05:21.060
about 10,900

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:23.170
some 46 times fewer.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:26.513
And note what the expectation is over

00:05:26.513 --> 00:05:29.821
the algorithm's own random pivot choices

00:05:29.821 --> 00:05:31.003
not the input.

00:05:31.003 --> 00:05:34.311
No input is bad for randomized quicksort

00:05:34.311 --> 00:05:36.595
only unlucky coin flips are

00:05:36.595 --> 00:05:38.800
and they are very unlikely.

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:41.938
Which pivot rule turns sorted

00:05:42.026 --> 00:05:44.319
input into the worst case?

00:05:44.319 --> 00:05:46.171
A: the last element.

00:05:46.171 --> 00:05:48.024
B: a random element.

00:05:48.024 --> 00:05:51.992
C: the median of the first, center and last.

00:05:51.992 --> 00:05:53.580
Pause and decide.

00:05:57.160 --> 00:05:57.748
Only A.

00:05:57.748 --> 00:06:00.437
With C, if the input is sorted

00:06:00.521 --> 00:06:03.126
the median of the three samples

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:06.487
is the center element, the true median

00:06:06.487 --> 00:06:09.680
so every split is perfectly balanced.

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:13.324
But median-of-three is not worst-case-proof

00:06:13.398 --> 00:06:15.794
specially constructed inputs can

00:06:15.869 --> 00:06:17.890
still force quadratic time.

00:06:17.965 --> 00:06:19.911
Only randomization removes

00:06:19.986 --> 00:06:22.830
bad inputs altogether, in expectation.

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:26.217
Here are all the sorting algorithms

00:06:26.297 --> 00:06:27.420
of the course.

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:28.302
Quicksort

00:06:28.302 --> 00:06:31.350
n log n in the best and average case

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:34.558
quadratic in the worst; no extra array

00:06:34.558 --> 00:06:36.643
only the recursion stack

00:06:36.643 --> 00:06:39.530
which is log n deep in expectation.

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:41.886
One more column: stable.

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:45.230
A sort is stable if elements with equal

00:06:45.313 --> 00:06:47.654
keys keep their input order.

00:06:47.738 --> 00:06:50.580
That matters when you sort records

00:06:50.663 --> 00:06:52.920
by one field after another.

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:55.894
Quicksort is not stable.

00:06:55.894 --> 00:07:00.083
The slides' example sorts

00:07:00.083 --> 00:07:01.080
5, 1, 2, 1*

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:05.269
where the star only marks the second 1

00:07:05.269 --> 00:07:08.959
using the last element as the pivot.

00:07:08.959 --> 00:07:11.253
The pivot is 1*.

00:07:11.253 --> 00:07:14.046
No key is smaller than 1

00:07:14.046 --> 00:07:16.340
so next stays at 0

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:18.731
and the final swap sends

00:07:18.816 --> 00:07:20.687
1* to the front

00:07:20.687 --> 00:07:22.983
in front of the other 1.

00:07:22.983 --> 00:07:26.810
The program prints 1*, 1, 2, 5.

00:07:27.160 --> 00:07:31.492
A stable sort would give

00:07:31.492 --> 00:07:32.082
1, 1*, 2, 5.

00:07:32.082 --> 00:07:34.248
And a common pitfall

00:07:34.248 --> 00:07:38.383
quicksort is n log n only in expectation

00:07:38.383 --> 00:07:39.860
or on average.

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:43.207
If you need a guaranteed n log n

00:07:43.207 --> 00:07:46.160
or stability, use merge sort.

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:50.134
Last question: can any sort beat n log n?

00:07:50.216 --> 00:07:52.782
Not if the only thing it may do

00:07:52.865 --> 00:07:55.017
with keys is compare them

00:07:55.017 --> 00:07:56.590
as insertion sort

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:58.990
merge sort and quicksort do.

00:07:59.073 --> 00:08:02.632
Every comparison sort needs on the order of

00:08:02.715 --> 00:08:05.860
n log n comparisons in the worst case.

00:08:06.210 --> 00:08:09.533
Fix a comparison sort and an input size.

00:08:09.533 --> 00:08:12.357
Its behaviour is a decision tree

00:08:12.357 --> 00:08:14.766
each node is one comparison

00:08:14.766 --> 00:08:16.676
such as a < b

00:08:16.676 --> 00:08:20.165
the two edges are the answers yes and no

00:08:20.165 --> 00:08:23.405
and each leaf is a final sorted order.

00:08:23.405 --> 00:08:24.734
For 3 keys

00:08:24.734 --> 00:08:27.890
insertion sort's tree has 6 leaves

00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:30.050
one for each permutation.

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:32.747
Running the algorithm follows one

00:08:32.818 --> 00:08:34.880
path from the root to a leaf

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:38.080
and the number of comparisons is its length.

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:41.280
The longest path here has 3 comparisons.

00:08:41.630 --> 00:08:44.577
Any tree that sorts must reach every

00:08:44.659 --> 00:08:47.115
one of the n! orders

00:08:47.115 --> 00:08:50.307
so it has at least n! leaves.

00:08:50.389 --> 00:08:52.763
A binary tree of height h has

00:08:52.845 --> 00:08:55.137
at most 2^h leaves.

00:08:55.219 --> 00:08:57.265
So the height is at least

00:08:57.347 --> 00:08:59.230
log₂(n!).

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:03.487
And n! lies between

00:09:03.598 --> 00:09:08.509
(n/2)^(n/2) and n^n.

00:09:08.509 --> 00:09:13.420
So log₂(n!) is Θ(n log n).

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:14.883
The numbers:

00:09:14.956 --> 00:09:17.383
3 keys need 3 comparisons

00:09:17.456 --> 00:09:18.780
in the worst case

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:21.280
and the tree we drew achieves it.

00:09:21.630 --> 00:09:24.564
1000 keys need at least

00:09:24.648 --> 00:09:27.078
8,530.

00:09:27.078 --> 00:09:28.084
Merge sort

00:09:28.084 --> 00:09:31.521
and randomized quicksort in expectation

00:09:31.521 --> 00:09:34.622
are optimal up to a constant factor.

00:09:34.706 --> 00:09:37.472
Algorithms that beat n log n must

00:09:37.556 --> 00:09:40.070
look at more than comparisons.

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:42.648
To sum up the lecture.

00:09:42.648 --> 00:09:46.462
One: quicksort's worst case is quadratic

00:09:46.462 --> 00:09:48.187
and its best case

00:09:48.187 --> 00:09:51.729
like any split by a constant fraction

00:09:51.729 --> 00:09:52.819
is n log n.

00:09:52.819 --> 00:09:55.089
Two: with random pivots

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:58.540
the expected number of comparisons is

00:09:58.631 --> 00:10:01.446
2(n+1)H_n

00:10:01.446 --> 00:10:04.170
minus 4n, on every input.

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:08.148
Three: quicksort is in place but not stable

00:10:08.231 --> 00:10:11.859
and no comparison sort can beat

00:10:11.942 --> 00:10:15.240
log₂(n!) comparisons in the worst case.

00:10:15.590 --> 00:10:16.288
Try this

00:10:16.288 --> 00:10:19.158
in a run on 100 distinct keys

00:10:19.235 --> 00:10:22.726
what is the probability that the smallest and

00:10:22.803 --> 00:10:25.673
the second smallest key are compared?

00:10:25.673 --> 00:10:28.310
And the smallest and the largest?

00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:32.061
Next lecture: counting sort and radix sort

00:10:32.140 --> 00:10:35.778
which beat n log n by looking at the digits of

00:10:35.857 --> 00:10:39.020
the keys instead of only comparing them.

